# Global Historical & Folklore Geographic Archive

## System Architecture

Version:
0.1 — Initial Architecture Definition

Status:
Prototype Planning Phase

---

# Overview

The archive is designed as a layered research system.

Each layer has a specific purpose while remaining connected to the others.

The architecture separates:

- Original sources
- Structured records
- Geographic information
- Historical context
- Knowledge relationships
- Research analysis

This allows the system to expand without changing its fundamental structure.

---

# Core Architecture Model

The system consists of the following layers:
Source Layer

↓

Data Organization Layer

↓

Geographic and Historical Context Layer

↓

Knowledge Relationship Layer

↓

Exploration and Analysis Layer

---

# Source Layer

The Source Layer contains original materials from which records are created.

Examples:

- Newspaper articles
- Books
- Archival documents
- Military records
- Maritime logs
- Interviews
- Oral tradition collections

Sources preserve:

- Original content
- Creator information
- Date
- Language
- Archive reference
- Quality information

A source is never replaced by a summary.

---

# Data Organization Layer

This layer converts source material into structured records.

A record may represent:

- Incident
- Tradition
- Observation
- Historical account
- Research note

Records maintain connections to:

- Original sources
- Geographic information
- Historical context
- Related records
- Research notes

---

# Geographic and Historical Context Layer

This layer provides spatial and temporal information.

It supports:

- Locations
- Historical place names
- Changing political boundaries
- Terrain
- Geological features
- Environmental conditions

Locations are treated as historical entities rather than fixed points.

---

# Knowledge Relationship Layer

This layer connects information together.

Examples:

- Similar reports
- Possible duplicates
- Shared locations
- Cultural connections
- Source lineage
- Historical continuity

Relationships are stored separately so they can evolve without altering original evidence.

---

# Exploration and Analysis Layer

This is the researcher-facing environment.

Capabilities include:

- Interactive maps
- Timelines
- Search
- Filtering
- Record comparison
- Geographic analysis
- Relationship visualization

The system supports discovery without forcing conclusions.

---

# Architecture Principle

The archive stores evidence.

The researcher explores relationships.


